# Firstmate's own critical surfaces, for bin/fm-lane-lib.sh.
#
# Format: "<glob> <surface-name>", one per line. Globs are matched
# case-insensitively against the repo-relative path of every changed file.
# This file only ADDS to the built-in catalog in bin/fm-lane-lib.sh; it can
# never remove a built-in surface, so nothing here can lower rigor.
#
# WEAKENING IS NEVER PORTABLE. STRENGTHENING IS. That is what the additive-only
# contract above means in the direction that matters: this file may raise THIS
# project's rigor as far as it likes, and nothing written here may lower anyone
# else's. The corollary is the one that was learned the hard way - a gap here is
# never grounds for narrowing the portable catalog to match. Widening an entry
# below is free; relaxing a built-in surface so a local label reads better is a
# FLEET DECISION, and a fleet decision is not made inside a delivery titled for
# something else. Ask what the change does in a project that declares nothing of
# its own: this file does not exist there.
#
# What belongs here: the paths whose names the portable built-in patterns do not
# already catch, but whose failure mode is a safety boundary rather than a bug -
# work discarded, a branch landed that should not have, a guard stepped around,
# a lock or lease that stops protecting what it names. Add a path here when it
# joins that set; never remove one to make a delivery cheaper.
#
# It also carries TWO boundaries this house raises for itself rather than imposing
# them on every project through the portable catalog. The first is this repo's CI
# configuration.
# The reason is not a prediction that CI would catch a bad change - that
# reasoning is rejected outright and belongs nowhere in this policy. It is that
# THE CHEAP LANE'S SAFETY NET CANNOT BE MODIFIABLE BY THE CHEAP LANE: cutting the
# net is not something a delivery gets to do without review.
#
# The second is the documents that govern this house and the instruction material
# a skill loads, which is the largest group below.
#
# Every group states its own reasoning AT THE GROUP, so this header deliberately
# does not enumerate them: an index here would be one more copy to fall behind,
# and the entries themselves are the only complete list. Read down.

bin/fm-gate-refuse-lib.sh security-guards
bin/fm-arm-pretool-check.sh security-guards
bin/fm-arm-command-policy.mjs security-guards
