# The policy's own enforcement points. The built-in catalog protects the files
# that DECLARE the routing policy; these are the two that RUN its dispatch gate,
# and a delivery that deleted the gate adds no lines, so every diff signal would
# read cheap and it would route itself past the gate it just removed.
#
# These two are the only thing protecting the gate, so nothing else may be
# assumed to cover them. The surface NAME also carries real information: a row in
# the record saying routing-policy tells whoever reads it in six months that the
# delivery touched the policy's own enforcement point.
bin/fm-spawn.sh routing-policy
bin/fm-promote.sh routing-policy

.github/workflows/* ci-configuration

# The documents that govern THIS HOUSE. A document that INSTRUCTS an agent or a
# contributor is never "pure documentation" for routing, because changing it
# changes behaviour, and the docs-only class would otherwise carry it to the
# cheap lane. A document that merely DESCRIBES is not a governing document.
#
# THAT IS DECIDED BY NAME, NEVER BY LOCATION. Every entry below is a BASENAME
# glob, so a governing document is one wherever it sits - docs/ is not a shelter,
# and docs/supervision-protocols/claude.md is a governing document today. There
# is no location rule in this policy and a description that implies one is how a
# reader ends up surprised.
#
# NOT A GOVERNING DOCUMENT IS NOT THE SAME AS CHEAP. This group decides one
# question only, and every other surface in the effective catalog still asks its
# own: the portable built-in patterns match by name anywhere in the tree too, so
# a basename carrying guard, merge or auth routes expensive under ITS surface
# whatever this group says. Only a decide against the real catalog answers what a
# given path routes to - no comment here can, and none should try.
# These live here rather than in the portable catalog because a surface put
# there is imposed silently on every project, while declaring it here raises
# this house's rigor, stays visible, and decides for nobody else.
# AGENTS.md is already a built-in routing-policy surface; do not repeat it.
#
# NAME THE ROLE, NOT THE SPELLING. A file is not an instruction because it ends
# in .md; it is an instruction because something LOADS IT AS ONE. Every entry
# below names the document and lets the extension follow, so no entry here can
# fall behind the prose extensions bin/fm-lane-lib.sh accepts. Naming only the
# .md spelling was a live hole, not a latent one: .rst and .adoc are accepted
# prose today, so CONTRIBUTING.rst and CLAUDE.rst reached the CHEAP lane.
CONTRIBUTING governing-docs
CONTRIBUTING.* governing-docs
*/CONTRIBUTING governing-docs
*/CONTRIBUTING.* governing-docs
CLAUDE governing-docs
CLAUDE.* governing-docs
*/CLAUDE governing-docs
*/CLAUDE.* governing-docs
SKILL governing-docs
SKILL.* governing-docs
*/SKILL governing-docs
*/SKILL.* governing-docs

# The instruction material a skill LOADS, named by the role it plays rather than
# by which extension a file happens to carry. A skill's instructions are its
# SKILL.md above, everything under its references/, and any agent definition it
# ships that supplies a prompt. Naming the locations means a reference file added
# tomorrow is covered the day it lands, with nothing to keep in step.
#
# THOSE FILES ARE THE FLEET'S BEHAVIOUR. A change there does not break one task -
# it changes how every future session acts. Of everything this policy routes, it
# is the least able to go cheap, and naming SKILL.md alone left all of it on the
# cheap route: a reference document changes agent behaviour exactly as its
# SKILL.md does.
#
# Measured against the tree rather than carried over from a report: 21 SKILL.md
# files, 12 files under references/, and 1 agent definition
# (.agents/skills/firstmate-codexapp/agents/openai.yaml, which carries a
# default_prompt), so 34 tracked instruction files, of which the 13 that are not
# SKILL.md previously kept the cheap route. One tracked file under a skill is
# deliberately NOT covered: .agents/skills/bearings/assets/board-template.html is
# a rendered output template, not instruction - it is what a skill PRODUCES, not
# what an agent is told.
skills/*/references/* governing-docs
*/skills/*/references/* governing-docs
skills/*/agents/* governing-docs
*/skills/*/agents/* governing-docs

# AUTHORS, because this house holds it expensive on purpose rather than by
# accident. It is project governance and attribution: who speaks for this house,
# and to whom the work is credited.
#
# These four entries name AUTHORS IRRESPECTIVE OF EXTENSION, and that is the
# whole reason they are written this way: the prose-extension list in
# bin/fm-lane-lib.sh may gain a spelling at any time, and a local entry that
# enumerated spellings would fall behind it in the CHEAP direction. Widening here
# is free, so this side is deliberately the wider of the two.
#
# WHEN TWO THINGS MUST BE COUPLED AND THE COUPLING CANNOT BE AVOIDED, IT MUST
# FAIL CLOSED. If they fall out of sync, the result must be MORE expensive, never
# cheaper. A coupling that cheapens when it breaks is a trap with a date on it:
# it will be sprung by someone doing something perfectly reasonable in another
# file, and it will lower rigor silently when it is. Here desynchronising costs
# one unnecessary expensive review, which is the safe side.
#
# THE PORTABLE *auth* GLOB STILL CLAIMS AUTHORS IN SPELLINGS OUTSIDE THE ACCEPTED
# PROSE LIST, deliberately, so AUTHORS.org records authentication ALONGSIDE
# governing-docs here and one of those two names is not true of it. That residual
# is an ACCEPTED TRADEOFF and fm_lane_authors_spelling in bin/fm-lane-lib.sh owns
# the reasoning - read it there before touching either side, because tidying the
# label once meant narrowing a portable surface and that measurably removed the
# authentication surface from src/authors.py in EVERY project the fleet routes.
# FIX THE LABEL, NOT THE ROUTING. These entries are not redundant with that glob:
# they are what holds AUTHORS expensive in the prose spellings the glob correctly
# lets through, and nothing anywhere else does that.
AUTHORS governing-docs
*/AUTHORS governing-docs
AUTHORS.* governing-docs
*/AUTHORS.* governing-docs
